          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            By a
            %em bireflective subcategory
            one might mean:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ι
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota \,\colon\, \mathcal{C} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{B}
                which is both
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategory"} coreflective
                — i.e. a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful functor
                possessing both
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left
                and
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoints"} right adjoints
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi ι
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \dashv \iota \dashv R
                (
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+localization"} reflective localization
                and
                = succeed ")." do
                  %em coreflective localization
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pair"} adjoint pair
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ι
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi L
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi ι
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota \circ L \dashv \iota \circ R
                is then an
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modality
            %li
              %p
                More specifically, one may in addition ask that
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \simeq R
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ι
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota
                has an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambidextrous+adjoint"} ambidextrous adjoint
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mi ι
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mi β
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta \dashv \iota \dashv \beta
              %p
                Such subcategories are called
                %strong quintessential localizations
                by
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#JS96"} Johnstone (1996)
              %p
                The corresponding (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"}> co
                \-)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ♮
                      %mo ≔
                      %mi ι
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi β
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \natural \coloneqq \iota \circ \beta
                is then a
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monad"} Frobenius monad
                (see at
                = succeed ")." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+modality"} classical modality
            %li
              %p
                Finally, one may moreover ask that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points-to-pieces+transform"} points-to-pieces transform
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ♮
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi ϵ
                      %mi id
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi η
                      %mo ♮
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \natural \overset{\epsilon}{\longrightarrow} id \overset{\eta}{\longrightarrow} \natural
                of a quintessential localization be the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphisms"} identity morphisms
              %p
                This strong form of the notion is the case actually called
                %strong bireflective subcategories
                by
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#FOPTST99"} Freyd et al. (1999)
          %h2#AmbidextrouslyBireflectiveSubcategories Definition
          %p
